From: pg_prosupport
Subject: Add agg_simplify_hook, a planner hook for plan-time Aggref rewrites

pg_prosupport's aggregate rewrites (numeric_support.c, constagg.c) need
one thing from the planner that a stock PostgreSQL 18 does not offer: a
place to intercept an Aggref while it is still in the query tree, before
the planner has split or otherwise committed to it, and hand back a
replacement.

This patch adds exactly that, as a single global hook rather than a
catalog-driven dispatch:

  - agg_simplify_hook_type / agg_simplify_hook in
    src/include/optimizer/clauses.h -- a plain function-pointer hook,
    in the same family as planner_hook, set_rel_pathlist_hook,
    join_search_hook and the rest;
  - a T_Aggref case in eval_const_expressions_mutator()
    (src/backend/optimizer/util/clauses.c) that calls it, immediately
    after the Aggref's own arguments have been simplified, and
    substitutes whatever non-NULL Node it returns.

A loaded module installs the hook once, in its _PG_init(), the same way
any other planner hook is installed (chaining the previous value, the
standard pattern for a hook more than one module might want). There is
no per-aggregate attachment, no catalog write, and no equivalent of
pg_proc.prosupport: the hook fires for every Aggref in every query, and
it is entirely up to the installed hook function to recognise, from
aggref->aggfnoid, which aggregates (if any) it wants to touch and leave
everything else alone by returning NULL. pg_prosupport's own hook
function (pps_simplify_aggref() in numeric_support.c) does exactly
that: two Oid comparisons decide sum(numeric)/avg(numeric) from
everything else, and everything else is the overwhelming majority of
calls.

This is deliberately simpler than PostgreSQL 19's own
SupportRequestSimplifyAggref/get_func_support() mechanism (commit
42473b3b31): no new node type, no pg_proc.prosupport lookup per Aggref,
nothing that depends on ALTER FUNCTION/pg_proc semantics at all. The
price is that the substitution logic has to live in a loaded module
rather than being reachable by writing one catalog field on a stock
server -- the module now has to be loaded before planning happens
(shared_preload_libraries, session_preload_libraries, or LOAD; see the
top-level README) rather than lazily on first use.

All 231 tests in `make check` under src/test/regress pass unchanged
against a tree with this patch applied: agg_simplify_hook is NULL on a
stock server, so the added branch is a single predicted-not-taken check
per Aggref and nothing else changes.

Tested against PostgreSQL 18.6. Apply from the top of a PostgreSQL
source tree with:

    patch -p1 < contrib/pg_prosupport/patches/pg18-agg-simplify-hook.patch

or, inside a git checkout:

    git apply contrib/pg_prosupport/patches/pg18-agg-simplify-hook.patch

Then build PostgreSQL as usual and build/install pg_prosupport against
that build; numeric_agg and const_agg (`make installcheck`) exercise
the hook end to end.
---
diff --git a/src/backend/optimizer/util/clauses.c b/src/backend/optimizer/util/clauses.c
index aa70667..c8d5835 100644
--- a/src/backend/optimizer/util/clauses.c
+++ b/src/backend/optimizer/util/clauses.c
@@ -94,6 +94,14 @@ typedef struct
 	List	   *safe_param_ids; /* PARAM_EXEC Param IDs to treat as safe */
 } max_parallel_hazard_context;

+/*
+ * Hook for plugins to take over simplification of an Aggref at plan time;
+ * see the comment on agg_simplify_hook_type in optimizer/clauses.h.  NULL
+ * means no plugin is interested, which is the state on a stock server: the
+ * check costs one predicted-not-taken branch per Aggref.
+ */
+agg_simplify_hook_type agg_simplify_hook = NULL;
+
 /*
  * Walker context for expression_has_grouping_conflict.  get_eqop is a callback
  * that returns the equality operator used for grouping.  cb_context is opaque
@@ -2647,6 +2655,21 @@ eval_const_expressions_mutator(Node *node,
 				newexpr->location = expr->location;
 				return (Node *) newexpr;
 			}
+		case T_Aggref:
+			{
+				node = ece_generic_processing(node);
+
+				if (agg_simplify_hook)
+				{
+					Node	   *newnode;
+
+					newnode = agg_simplify_hook(context->root, (Aggref *) node);
+					if (newnode != NULL)
+						return newnode;
+				}
+
+				return node;
+			}
 		case T_OpExpr:
 			{
 				OpExpr	   *expr = (OpExpr *) node;
diff --git a/src/include/optimizer/clauses.h b/src/include/optimizer/clauses.h
index 5935c50..0c0ee32 100644
--- a/src/include/optimizer/clauses.h
+++ b/src/include/optimizer/clauses.h
@@ -33,6 +33,27 @@ typedef struct
  */
 typedef Oid (*grouping_eqop_callback) (Var *var, void *context);

+/*
+ * Hook for plugins to take over simplification of an Aggref at plan time.
+ *
+ * eval_const_expressions_mutator() calls this, if set, for every Aggref it
+ * meets, immediately after simplifying the Aggref's own arguments.  The hook
+ * gets the current PlannerInfo and the Aggref itself; it must not modify the
+ * Aggref in place (build a copy if it wants to return a changed one -- see
+ * copyObject()).  Returning NULL leaves the Aggref alone; returning a
+ * non-NULL Node substitutes that node in its place, exactly like any other
+ * eval_const_expressions_mutator() rewrite.
+ *
+ * There is no catalog-driven dispatch here -- unlike a plain function's
+ * SupportRequestSimplify, which core looks up per-function via
+ * pg_proc.prosupport, this hook is a single global entry point that a
+ * loaded module installs in its _PG_init() and that fires for every
+ * Aggref in every query; the hook function itself has to recognise which
+ * aggregate (if any) it wants to touch, from aggref->aggfnoid.
+ */
+typedef Node *(*agg_simplify_hook_type) (PlannerInfo *root, Aggref *aggref);
+extern PGDLLIMPORT agg_simplify_hook_type agg_simplify_hook;
+
 extern bool contain_agg_clause(Node *clause);

 extern bool contain_window_function(Node *clause);
